# Talking Receptionist — Decision + Prompts (9:16 portrait)

## Decision: SadTalker for speaking, Gemini/Veo (or Grok) for silent loops

These solve different problems — use both, not one instead of the other:

- **Speaking state → SadTalker** (or Wav2Lip if you want lighter/faster). These take
  a single still photo + a real audio file and generate lips that match that exact
  audio, sample-accurate. That's what you need: your TTS engine produces the audio,
  SadTalker renders a video whose mouth actually matches those words. Gemini/Veo-style
  text-to-video generates its own guessed mouth motion — it is not driven by your TTS
  audio, so it will drift out of sync with what the receptionist is actually saying.
  Not usable for a state that must match generated speech.
- **Idle / thinking / welcome / thank_you → Gemini (Veo) or Grok video-gen**, since
  these are silent, scripted loops/gestures with no audio to match. Either engine is
  fine here; pick whichever is fastest for you to iterate with.

Fastest path to a working demo:
1. Generate ONE reference photo/frame of the receptionist (or the first frame of your
   Gemini/Grok idle.mp4) — reuse it as the source image for SadTalker so the face
   stays identical across the speaking clip and the idle/gesture clips.
2. Run SadTalker locally against that photo + a sample TTS audio clip to produce
   `speaking.mp4` (or generate it live, per-utterance, at demo time — see below).
3. Generate `idle.mp4`, `thinking.mp4`, `welcome.mp4`, `thank_you.mp4` via Gemini/Grok
   using the master prompt below, seeded/reference-imaged off the same source photo.

### Running SadTalker (speaking state)

```bash
git clone https://github.com/OpenTalker/SadTalker.git
cd SadTalker
conda create -n sadtalker python=3.8 -y && conda activate sadtalker
pip install -r requirements.txt
bash scripts/download_models.sh   # or the Windows equivalent in their README

python inference.py \
  --driven_audio ./examples/driven_audio/tts_output.wav \
  --source_image ./examples/source_image/receptionist.png \
  --result_dir ./results \
  --still --preprocess full --enhancer gfpgan
```

- `--driven_audio` is the WAV your TTS step just produced for that turn.
- `--still` keeps head movement minimal so it matches the "locked tripod" framing
  of the other clips.
- `--enhancer gfpgan` sharpens the face; drop it if you need faster turnaround.
- For live, per-utterance generation at runtime (not pre-baked), call this same
  command from your backend right after TTS finishes, then stream/serve the
  resulting mp4 to the frontend crossfader below. Expect several seconds of
  render time on CPU — a GPU (even a modest one) is strongly recommended if you
  want this in the live demo loop rather than pre-generated.
- If render time is too slow for a live demo, fall back to **Wav2Lip** (faster,
  slightly lower fidelity) with the same source photo, or pre-generate a small
  library of `speaking_*.mp4` loops and pick the closest-length one instead of
  generating on the fly.

